---
import GuideLayout from "@layouts/GuideLayout.astro";

const title = "Owner-Retained Total Loss & Salvage Titles Explained | FairClaimCalculator";
const description =
  "What happens if you keep your totaled vehicle: how the salvage-value deduction works, what a salvage or rebuilt title means, and how it affects value long-term.";
const path = "/guides/total-loss/owner-retained-total-loss-and-salvage-titles/";
---
<GuideLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  h1="Owner-Retained Total Loss & Salvage Titles"
  cluster={{ name: "Total Loss", href: "/guides/total-loss/" }}
  publishedDate="2026-07-29"
  reviewedDate="2026-07-29"
  summary="If you keep your totaled vehicle instead of surrendering it, the insurer deducts its salvage value from your payout, and your state issues a salvage title — a permanent record that follows the car for life."
  sources={[
    'Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salvage_title" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Salvage title</a> — general overview; branding criteria differ by state and should be confirmed with your state DMV.',
    'New York State Department of Financial Services, <a href="https://www.dfs.ny.gov/insurance/ogco2000/rg000207.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OGC Opinion No. 00-02-07: Salvage Vehicle Branding</a> (primary — a real regulator opinion letter on salvage-title branding).',
  ]}
  relatedLinks={[
    { label: "Settlement Check Breakdown calculator", href: "/calculators/settlement-check-breakdown/" },
    { label: "ACV vs. Replacement Cost vs. Loan Payoff vs. Asking Price", href: "/guides/total-loss/acv-vs-replacement-cost-loan-payoff-asking-price/" },
  ]}
>
  <h2>What "owner retention" means</h2>
  <p>
    When your car is declared a total loss, you generally have two options: surrender it to the
    insurer (who sells it for salvage), or keep it yourself ("owner retain" or "owner buyback"). If
    you keep it, the insurer deducts an estimated salvage value from your ACV before paying you —
    since they aren't taking possession of, and reselling, the wreck.
  </p>

  <div class="example-box">
    <p>
      <strong>Worked example.</strong> ACV is $16,000. The insurer estimates salvage value (what
      the wrecked vehicle itself is worth, e.g., to a salvage buyer or for parts) at $3,200. If you
      retain the vehicle, your payout is $16,000 − $3,200 = <strong>$12,800</strong>, and you keep
      the car with a branded title.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Salvage title vs. rebuilt title</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Salvage title:</strong> issued once a vehicle is declared a total loss. In most
      states, a salvage-titled vehicle cannot legally be driven on public roads until it's repaired
      and re-inspected.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rebuilt title:</strong> issued after a previously salvage-titled vehicle is repaired
      and passes your state's required inspection, allowing it back on the road.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="state-note">
    <strong>What varies by state:</strong> the exact criteria for issuing a salvage title, the
    inspection process for a rebuilt title, and whether a title is branded at all for a given
    damage level, all differ by state. Confirm your state's specific process with your state's DMV
    or motor vehicle agency before assuming your vehicle can (or can't) be returned to the road.
  </p>

  <h2>The permanent effect on value</h2>
  <p>
    A salvage or rebuilt brand does not go away — it stays attached to the vehicle's history
    through services like Carfax and the National Motor Vehicle Title Information System (NMVTIS)
    even after professional repairs. This is a separate concept from — and often confused with —
    <em>diminished value</em>, which is specifically about a vehicle that was <strong>repaired,
    not totaled</strong>, and did not receive a salvage title. See
    <a href="/guides/diminished-value/what-is-diminished-value/">What Diminished Value Means</a> for
    that distinction.
  </p>

  <h2>What this guide cannot tell you</h2>
  <p>
    It cannot estimate your specific vehicle's salvage value, tell you whether your state allows
    owner retention for your damage level, or predict your state's rebuilt-title inspection
    requirements — all of these depend on your state and your insurer's own process.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
